  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1224 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nPWATs climb to the 1.00\" mark Saturday on their way higher to 1.10\" \nto 1.20\" by Sunday and Monday, thanks to the monsoon high sliding \neastward toward the TX border by Saturday. This will allow for a \nplume of monsoon moisture to advect northward into the Desert \nSouthwest focused thru eastern AZ and western NM. Daily rounds of \nafternoon showers and thunderstorms will favor this plume across \nwestern and northwestern NM Saturday thru at least Monday. Any \nflooding potential across western NM will be limited by storm \nmotions being just fast enough, but favoring areas where storms \ntrain over the same location repeatedly. The Rio Grande Valley and \nareas along the central mountain chain will be in a transition zone \nwith the best chances for thunderstorm activity late in the \nafternoon finding its way eastward from the Continental Divide. One \narea of lower forecast certainty will be Sunday's convective \npotential with the likelihood for cloud cover persisting late \nSaturday night thru Sunday morning. This would limit and delay \ndestabilization for convective initiation to begin. Meanwhile, \ntemperatures begin to lower thru western NM, staying hot \nnear the 100F mark for the eastern plains.",
